Object.assign(window.deepArticles, {
  "united-states-aerospace-force": {
    title:"United States Aerospace Force",category:"Military",eyebrow:"Independent service · strategic aviation · orbital operations",infoboxKicker:"United States armed service",infoboxTitle:"United States Aerospace Force",
    lead:"The United States Aerospace Force is the independent armed service responsible for continental interception, strategic strike, long-range reconnaissance, missiles, military spaceplanes, orbital security, and deep-space military systems. It initially operates the national scientific and transport program because no separate civil space agency exists. After 1990 licensed companies assume much routine transport and logistics while the service retains traffic authority, protected command, strategic maneuver, and combat missions.",
    canon:"The 1958 command, 1962 independent service, four-command space organization, strategic functions, runway-to-orbit fleet, military command of Columbia and Kennedy Station, post-1990 commercial transfer, survival of reduced O-Hundred programs, return to F/A/B designations for orbit-only combat craft, and orbital-carrier requirement are established. Lower formations, ranks below senior command, most base names, accepted combat-craft designations, and whether an orbital carrier reached authorization or construction by 5 January 2012 remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"origins",title:"Origins in defeat and technological shock",html:"<p>Before the Philippine War, strategic bombers and experimental rockets answer to separate service bureaux and a small joint board. The difficulty of coordinating reconnaissance, high-altitude interception, missiles, and expeditionary air power makes that arrangement visibly inadequate.</p><p>Germany's 1957 satellite produces the decisive shock. Washington establishes United States Aerospace Command in 1958 to join strategic interception, bombers, missiles, high-altitude research, and military space planning. Its <a href='american-reusable-aerospace-research-generation.html'>X-15, Dyna-Soar, X-24, and Apex research paths</a> establish the experimental foundation for runway-to-orbit flight.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"independence",title:"Independent service, 1962",html:"<p>President Kennedy elevates the command into an independent service in 1962. The Army Air Corps retains battlefield aviation, assault lift, and organic reconnaissance; the Naval Air Corps retains carrier and maritime aviation. The Aerospace Force receives the national strategic and orbital mission.</p><p>Aircraft serving a battlefield belong to the Army, aircraft serving a fleet to the Navy, and systems acting across continents or orbits to the Aerospace Force. The service's <a href='american-aerospace-force-atmospheric-fleet.html'>atmospheric fleet</a> accordingly joins continental interceptors, strategic reconnaissance, aerospace training, and conventional high-speed bombers under one command.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"commands",title:"The four space commands",html:"<p>Aerospace Development Command manages experimental vehicles, propulsion, materials, thermal protection, flight computers, and procurement. Orbital Transportation Command operates the runway-to-orbit fleet, traffic control, aerospace fields, and routine transport.</p><p>Space Science and Development Command operates Columbia's research sections, Kennedy Lunar Station, and planetary missions with universities and contractors. Orbital Security Command controls <a href='project-zenith.html'>Project Zenith</a>, military reconnaissance, armed inspection, strategic orbital operations, and protected facilities.</p>"},
      {id:"civil",title:"Science inside a military service",html:"<p>The United States never creates a NASA-like independent civil agency. Civilian scientists, universities, companies, and foreign guests initially participate through Space Science and Development Command. Mission authorization, traffic, rescue, communications, and emergency life-support authority remain military.</p><p>Columbia begins as open military infrastructure used for civil purposes, while Zenith is closed military infrastructure. Licensed operators and commercial modules assume a growing share of civil transport and logistics after 1985 without acquiring sovereign traffic or emergency authority.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"fields",title:"Aerospace fields",html:"<p>Canaveral Aerospace Field is the principal operational base, defined by a reinforced runway roughly ten to twelve kilometers long, Atlantic approach corridors, buried hydrogen and oxygen storage, thermal-protection hangars, mission control, and vehicle-sized aprons.</p><p>Edwards remains the principal test and western recovery field. Vandenberg supports high-inclination and polar operations. Emergency runways exist elsewhere, but few bases can fully service an orbital aircraft.</p>"},
      {id:"fleet",title:"Runway-to-orbit fleet",html:"<p>The Aerospace Force develops integrated multi-cycle vehicles that climb through turbine, ramjet, high-speed air-breathing, and closed-cycle rocket regimes without expendable external tanks or solid boosters. The approach is expensive and initially payload-poor, but it creates a reusable pilot-centered architecture.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-p5d7x writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T11:55:54-06:00 --><p>XO-1 Pathfinder proves orbital flight in 1969. O-2 Columbia supplies the cislunar expedition system. O-3 Liberty carries general traffic, while O-4 Hercules lifts heavy construction cargo. O-5 Ranger services and recovers spacecraft. O-6 Enterprise carries passengers at high frequency. O-7A Zenith adds rapid-launch interception, military maneuver, electronic warfare, protected navigation, and an internal mission bay.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"maracaibo",title:"Maracaibo and doctrinal retreat",html:"<p>The 1966 <a href='maracaibo-shock.html'>Maracaibo Shock</a> destroys the assumption that speed and altitude make strategic aircraft safe. The service invests in electronic warfare, decoys, threat libraries, route optimization, precision navigation, terrain following, and upper-atmosphere vehicles.</p><p>By the early 1980s new atmospheric bomber investment declines as orbital mobility and guided conventional reentry become the preferred high-end strategic direction.</p>"},
      {id:"politics",title:"Constitutional and political position",html:"<p>Congress funds scientific and military programs through the same service, making budget transparency difficult. Supporters argue that unified command prevented the fragmentation that delayed the country after 1941. Critics describe a military institution with unusual authority over science, public spectacle, industrial contracts, and the national image of the future.</p><p>The service's prestige survives the transfer from Buchanan to Reagan because both parties treat reusable aerospace, Columbia, lunar permanence, and strategic orbital capacity as national assets.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"deep-space",title:"Deep-space programs",html:"<p>XO-100 Pilgrim opens the Deep-Space Century as an orbit-built vehicle that normally does not enter Earth's atmosphere. The O-101 through O-109 block reserves personnel, freight, tanker, rescue, patrol, construction, survey, electric-freight, and endurance requirements.</p><p>The 1990 austerity crisis threatens several late requirements. The service preserves the deep-space mission through smaller fleets, delayed schedules, corporate participation, and commercial derivatives rather than accepting its abolition.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"commercial",title:"Commercial transition after 1990",html:"<p>Frontier and other licensed carriers take over scheduled personnel movement, freight, satellite service, propellant delivery, and portions of station construction. Established aviation manufacturers continue designing vehicles for government and private requirements.</p><p>The Aerospace Force retains ranges, traffic control, rescue coordination, treaty responsibility, strategic-payload review, emergency requisition, and military systems. Its relationship to private transport becomes regulatory, contractual, and reserve-based as well as operational.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"combat-service",title:"Combat service after 2000",html:"<p>Private transport allows the service to reduce its role as the national orbital airline. Procurement concentrates on military surveillance, secured command, strategic maneuver, defended infrastructure, and purpose-built orbit-only combat craft.</p><p>Fighter, attack, and bomber missions return to the ordinary F, A, and B designation sequences. The service continues to use the terms aircraft, pilot, squadron, and sortie for vehicles that cannot survive atmospheric entry.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"carrier",title:"Orbital carrier requirement",html:"<p>The carrier requirement combines command, sensors, propellant, magazines, maintenance, crew support, and rescue with a longitudinal hangar and paired handling bays for specialized combat craft. Protection uses layered debris and fragment shielding, local armor around critical systems, duplicated services, compartmentalization, and damage control.</p><p>The requirement creates direct budget competition with oceanic naval aviation. No commissioned carrier class, fixed hull name, or final construction status is established by 5 January 2012.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p>"}
    ],related:americanMissionRelated.filter(item=>item.href!=="united-states-aerospace-force.html").concat([{href:"aerospace-doctrine.html",kicker:"Service doctrine",label:"Aerospace Doctrine"},{href:"american-aerospace-force-atmospheric-fleet.html",kicker:"Atmospheric register",label:"Aerospace Force Atmospheric Fleet"},{href:"orbital-weapons.html",kicker:"Combat systems",label:"Orbital Weapons"},{href:"space-programs.html",kicker:"Program history",label:"Space Race"},{href:"orbital-stations.html",kicker:"Permanent orbit",label:"Orbital Stations"},{href:"united-states.html",kicker:"National setting",label:"United States"}]),sources:americanAerospaceSources,
    categories:["United States","Armed forces","Aerospace","Space program","Military space"],
    facts:[["Predecessor","United States Aerospace Command · 1958"],["Independent service","1962"],["Supreme authority","President and Congress"],["Principal domains","Strategic air · missiles · space"],["Civil program","Initially operated within the service; routine transport later commercialized"],["Main field","Canaveral Aerospace Field"],["Principal orbital post","Orbital Port Columbia"],["Military program","Project Zenith"],["Deep-space block","O-100 series"],["2000s combat register","F · A · B mission designations"],["Orbital carrier","Requirement established; class and status open"],["Reference date","5 January 2012"]]
  },

  "hemispheric-renewal-act": {
    title:"Hemispheric Renewal Act",category:"Politics",eyebrow:"Kennedy administration · 1961 · American mission state",infoboxKicker:"United States federal legislation",infoboxTitle:"Hemispheric Renewal Act of 1961",
    lead:"The Hemispheric Renewal Act was the central domestic and strategic program of John F. Kennedy's first year. It joined aerospace acceleration, military modernization, technical education, infrastructure, civil rights, and a more interventionist Monroe Doctrine into one claim: a republic that could not renew itself could not exclude victorious empires from the Americas.",
    canon:"The act's 1961 passage, mission framework, aerospace and educational program, civil-rights argument, and relationship to the interventions in Honduras and Venezuela are established. Exact appropriations, titles, and roll-call totals remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"background",title:"Background",html:"<p>Kennedy inherits delayed prosperity, the Philippine withdrawal, German orbital leadership, Japanese industrial growth, and a fractured party system. The act answers these as one crisis of national capacity rather than separate foreign and domestic problems.</p>"},
      {id:"program",title:"The renewal program",html:"<p>The legislation expands aerospace research, strategic aviation, technical colleges, mathematics and science teaching, transport and communications, industrial laboratories, and federal purchasing of advanced equipment. It also supports state and local modernization where projects can be described as part of continental resilience.</p>"},
      {id:"aerospace",title:"Aerospace acceleration",html:"<p>The act supplies the institutional and financial base for Kennedy's later lunar commitment and the 1962 creation of the <a href='united-states-aerospace-force.html'>United States Aerospace Force</a>. Reusable, pilot-centered flight becomes the program's public signature.</p>"},
      {id:"rights",title:"Civil rights as strategic legitimacy",html:"<p>Kennedy argues that foreign propaganda can exploit unequal citizenship and that American leadership in the hemisphere requires visible constitutional equality. Civil rights are therefore presented as both a moral duty and strategic defense.</p><p>The formula widens federal action while alarming Southern Democrats who reject the use of international rivalry to transform domestic law.</p>"},
      {id:"doctrine",title:"The renewed Monroe Doctrine",html:"<p>The act does not itself authorize every intervention, but its security provisions treat foreign military, communications, energy, and political penetration as one hemispheric problem. Assistance becomes conditional upon excluding Einheitspakt, Japanese, and Latin-bloc strategic influence.</p>"},
      {id:"honduras",title:"The Honduran test",html:"<p>The <a href='honduran-security-crisis.html'>Honduran Security Crisis</a> of 1962 becomes the first practical test. Rapid intervention and a dependent provisional order persuade the administration that early coercion close to American logistics can succeed where late control in the Philippines failed.</p>"},
      {id:"coalition",title:"Political coalition and opposition",html:"<p>Urban liberals, defense modernizers, technical educators, parts of organized labor, and National Renewal mission nationalists support different portions. Fiscal conservatives object to federal direction; noninterventionists reject the foreign-policy logic; the left warns that reform at home is becoming the moral language of empire abroad.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Legacy",html:"<p>The act gives Kennedy's presidency a coherent political vocabulary and establishes “renewal” as a term no later party can surrender. National Renewal praises its movement but condemns its constitutional restraint; Reagan later preserves its science, defense, and educational inheritance while criticizing mission administered through permanent political management.</p>"}
    ],related:americanMissionRelated.filter(item=>item.href!=="hemispheric-renewal-act.html").concat([{href:"presidents.html",kicker:"Executive history",label:"List of U.S. Presidents"},{href:"american-hemisphere.html",kicker:"Foreign-policy system",label:"American Hemisphere"},{href:"rocket-awakening.html",kicker:"Cultural setting",label:"Rocket Awakening"}]),sources:americanMissionSources,
    categories:["United States","John F. Kennedy","Federal legislation","American Hemisphere","Civil rights"],
    facts:[["Year","1961"],["President","John F. Kennedy"],["Core formula","Domestic renewal for hemispheric leadership"],["Strategic fields","Aerospace · communications · defense"],["Civil fields","Education · infrastructure · civil rights"],["Foreign doctrine","Interventionist Monroe system"],["First operational test","Honduras · 1962"],["Long-term legacy","Foundation of American mission politics"]]
  },

  "honduran-security-crisis": {
    title:"Honduran Security Crisis",category:"Conflicts",eyebrow:"1962 · American intervention · Central America",infoboxKicker:"Hemispheric intervention",infoboxTitle:"Honduran Security Crisis",
    lead:"The Honduran Security Crisis was the 1962 constitutional breakdown and American intervention that removed a national-corporatist government courted by Germany and the Latin Bloc. The short campaign created the first durable American client government after the Philippine failure and became the model for early, close, logistically overwhelming intervention.",
    canon:"The 1962 crisis, government alignment, American forces and methods, rapid victory, dependent successor regime, and doctrinal lesson are established. Exact Honduran leaders, unit names, dates, casualties, and constitutional instruments remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"background",title:"National-corporatist government",html:"<p>Honduras enters the 1960s with unstable civil-military politics, foreign fruit interests, labor conflict, and a governing movement sympathetic to corporatist political forms. The government moves toward emergency powers, a corporate charter, and technical-security agreements with German and Latin-bloc partners.</p>"},
      {id:"crisis",title:"Constitutional breakdown",html:"<p>Opposition officers, judges, labor organizations, and party leaders dispute the emergency program. Kennedy declares the incumbent government illegitimate and presents American action as restoration of constitutional order.</p><p>The strategic purpose is more direct: remove a foreign-aligned regime from the American security perimeter before outside personnel and infrastructure make intervention costly.</p>"},
      {id:"operation",title:"American intervention",html:"<p>Marines, light armor, tracked carriers, airborne or air-landed forces, naval blockade, radio-station seizures, and local opposition officers act in a tightly coordinated operation. Washington recognizes a provisional government as soon as the incumbent command structure collapses.</p>"},
      {id:"success",title:"Why the operation succeeds",html:"<p>Honduras is close to American bases, smaller than the Philippines, logistically dependent, politically divided, and beyond the ability of Germany or Italy to reinforce without risking direct war. The intervention occurs before the government can build a durable mass organization.</p><p>The lesson becomes: the Philippines failed because it was far away and late; Honduras succeeded because it was close and early.</p>"},
      {id:"client",title:"The dependent republic",html:"<p>Honduras retains a flag, constitution, elections, and formally sovereign ministries. In practice Washington controls security appointments, foreign military relations, strategic communications, and access to bases and transport corridors.</p><p>Local elites gain stability and American support in exchange for narrowing the range of permissible foreign and domestic policy.</p>"},
      {id:"reaction",title:"Domestic and international reaction",html:"<p>Kennedy receives a short political victory. Republicans divide between anti-German support and objections to executive overreach. National Renewal applauds action while mocking the language of constitutional restoration. The American left treats the operation as imperial government disguised as legality.</p><p>Germany condemns the removal of a sovereign government for its ideas. Italy and Spain protest more cautiously, recognizing that Washington is excluding Latin Catholic as well as German influence.</p>"},
      {id:"doctrine",title:"The Honduras doctrine",html:"<p>The campaign validates deployable light armor, tracked carriers, air-landed forces, radio seizure, blockade, provisional recognition, and reliance upon local officers. Honduras becomes a logistics and intelligence node for American policy toward Nicaragua, Guatemala, El Salvador, and the western Caribbean.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Legacy",html:"<p>The success makes later intervention more likely. It convinces ordinary voters that American power can impose order after the Philippine humiliation, while teaching neighboring states that constitutional form offers little protection when strategic alignment is judged unacceptable.</p><p>The bloodier <a href='venezuelan-intervention.html'>Venezuelan intervention</a> tests whether the Honduran method can survive a larger country with modern foreign equipment.</p>"}
    ],related:americanMissionRelated.filter(item=>item.href!=="honduran-security-crisis.html").concat([{href:"honduras.html",kicker:"National setting",label:"Republic of Honduras"},{href:"philippine-war.html",kicker:"Doctrinal contrast",label:"Philippine War"},{href:"american-hemisphere.html",kicker:"Regional order",label:"American Hemisphere"}]),sources:americanMissionSources,
    categories:["Honduras","United States","Military interventions","American Hemisphere","1962"],
    facts:[["Date","1962"],["Location","Honduras"],["American president","John F. Kennedy"],["Public justification","Restoration of constitutional order"],["Strategic purpose","Exclusion of rival-bloc influence"],["Principal forces","Marines · light armor · blockade · local officers"],["Outcome","American victory"],["Political result","De facto American client republic"]]
  },

  "venezuelan-intervention": {
    title:"Venezuelan Intervention",category:"Conflicts",eyebrow:"1966 · oil security · Kennedy's second term",infoboxKicker:"Hemispheric intervention",infoboxTitle:"American intervention in Venezuela",
    lead:"The Venezuelan Intervention was the American military action of 1966 that defeated a Venezuelan coalition seeking German, Japanese, and Latin-bloc technical participation in oil, communications, and defense. Unlike Honduras, Venezuelan forces fought with modern foreign-supported systems; the United States won but converted Venezuelan sovereignty into a conditional security settlement.",
    canon:"The oil and strategic dispute, 1966 intervention, genuine fighting, Maracaibo losses, American victory, occupation of strategic nodes, and Caracas Security Settlement are established. Exact leaders, operational dates, casualties beyond Maracaibo, and treaty articles remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"oil",title:"The oil-service dispute",html:"<p>A German–Japanese consortium offers to build and operate extraction facilities, refineries, pipelines, communications, and training in exchange for a defined production share. Caracas describes the arrangement as purchasing technical service while returning most earnings to Venezuela.</p><p>Washington sees foreign technicians gaining physical control of the Caribbean oil heartland and the infrastructure through which its production can be stopped or redirected.</p>"},
      {id:"division",title:"Venezuelan division",html:"<p>Nationalists support the concession as an escape from American corporate dependence. Pro-American officers believe it invites invasion and begin conspiring. Foreign equipment, technical guards, advisers, and communications systems appear around selected facilities.</p>"},
      {id:"intervention",title:"The intervention",html:"<p>Kennedy commits open force during his second term. American aircraft, naval forces, Marines, airborne units, intelligence teams, and Venezuelan collaborators seize coastal approaches and isolate the central command.</p><p>Venezuelan units resist rather than dissolve. Modern radar and surface-to-air weapons impose real losses and prevent the campaign from becoming another short Honduran demonstration.</p>"},
      {id:"maracaibo",title:"Maracaibo",html:"<p>Near Maracaibo, German-designed air-defense systems anticipate a high-altitude American route and destroy two B-58 bombers and a suppression aircraft. The <a href='maracaibo-shock.html'>Maracaibo Shock</a> becomes the campaign's most important technical event even though it does not prevent American victory.</p>"},
      {id:"occupation",title:"Strategic occupation",html:"<p>American forces seize major airfields, coastal oil terminals, refineries, command centers, and Caracas communications. Direct formal occupation is kept short; control moves rapidly to a provisional Venezuelan authority supported by American advisers.</p>"},
      {id:"settlement",title:"Caracas Security Settlement",html:"<p>The settlement restores elections while reserving American review of foreign oil operating agreements, naval and air access, advisers within defense and intelligence ministries, supervision of strategic communications, and a right of renewed intervention against extra-hemispheric penetration.</p><p>Venezuela remains a republic whose sovereignty is conditional on the strategic questions Washington considers essential.</p>"},
      {id:"politics",title:"Collaboration and grievance",html:"<p>Later Venezuelan politics divides over who invited the Americans, who collaborated after intervention, and whether restored electoral government can legitimate restrictions imposed by foreign force. Oil revenue and American market access make complete rupture costly.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Hemispheric legacy",html:"<p>The victory tightens the American security corridor through the Caribbean and northern South America. It also alarms Canada, Britain, Cuba, Mexico, and the Latin states by proving that Washington will use open force against a larger republic when strategic infrastructure is at stake.</p><p>Germany concludes both that America will enforce its own sphere and that American premier weapons can be defeated by an integrated technical system.</p>"}
    ],related:americanMissionRelated.filter(item=>item.href!=="venezuelan-intervention.html").concat([{href:"venezuela.html",kicker:"National setting",label:"Republic of Venezuela"},{href:"american-hemisphere.html",kicker:"Regional order",label:"American Hemisphere"},{href:"iceland-crisis.html",kicker:"Next confrontation",label:"Iceland Crisis"}]),sources:americanMissionSources.concat([{href:"../aerospaceArmamentsReference%20-%20AltWWII.md",label:"Aerospace Armaments Reference — Maracaibo losses and doctrinal response"}]),
    categories:["Venezuela","United States","Military interventions","Oil politics","1966"],
    facts:[["Date","1966"],["Location","Venezuela"],["Cause","Foreign control of oil and strategic systems"],["American president","John F. Kennedy"],["Principal technical event","Maracaibo Shock"],["Outcome","American military victory"],["Settlement","Caracas Security Settlement"],["Later status","Electoral republic under conditional sovereignty"]]
  },

  "maracaibo-shock": {
    title:"Maracaibo Shock",category:"Military history",eyebrow:"1966 · integrated air defense · doctrinal change",infoboxKicker:"Aerospace engagement",infoboxTitle:"Maracaibo Shock",
    lead:"The Maracaibo Shock was the destruction of two American B-58 strategic bombers and one suppression aircraft by German-designed Venezuelan air defenses during the 1966 intervention. It ended the American belief that speed and altitude alone could guarantee penetration and redirected strategic investment toward electronic warfare, low-level routing, precision navigation, and orbital systems.",
    canon:"The 1966 setting, three American losses, German-designed integrated radar and missile system, and principal doctrinal consequences are fixed. Unit identities, missile type, crews, exact date, and precise engagement geometry remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"mission",title:"The high-altitude mission",html:"<p>American planners assign B-58 aircraft and a suppression escort to a route near Maracaibo, relying upon speed, altitude, and the assumption that Venezuelan operators cannot integrate foreign equipment quickly enough to predict the approach.</p>"},
      {id:"system",title:"The defending system",html:"<p>The defense is not one miraculous missile. German-designed search radar, tracking, fire control, communications, doctrine, technical personnel, and local operators form an integrated system able to estimate the American route and prepare engagement windows.</p>"},
      {id:"losses",title:"The engagement",html:"<p>Two B-58 bombers and one suppression aircraft are destroyed. The losses are militarily limited inside the larger campaign and psychologically enormous because the aircraft represent the most visible claim that the upper atmosphere belongs to American speed.</p>"},
      {id:"america",title:"American response",html:"<p>The Aerospace Force expands electronic-warfare analysis, threat libraries, decoys, route optimization, terrain following, precision inertial navigation, digital mission planning, and upper-atmosphere research. Suppression becomes a continuing systems contest rather than a preliminary attack assumed to succeed.</p>"},
      {id:"germany",title:"German interpretation",html:"<p>German planners conclude that American superiority is technical rather than mystical and can therefore be studied and defeated. Export air defense gains prestige, while Wolfgang takes the engagement as proof that Washington may be resisted without Germany sending state forces into the hemisphere.</p>"},
      {id:"strategic",title:"From bombers to aerospace vehicles",html:"<p>Maracaibo does not immediately end the strategic bomber. It alters the direction of prestige investment. Reusable aerospace vehicles promise flexible routing, orbital maneuver, reconnaissance, inspection, and guided conventional reentry outside the familiar geometry of atmospheric defense.</p><p>The line runs from Pathfinder through Columbia to <a href='project-zenith.html'>Project Zenith</a>.</p>"},
      {id:"politics",title:"Political meaning",html:"<p>Supporters of Kennedy emphasize that America absorbed the loss and still won Venezuela. Critics see proof that intervention placed elite crews at risk for control of another country's oil and diplomacy. National Renewal argues that technological humiliation requires still greater national discipline.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Legacy",html:"<p>“Another Maracaibo” becomes shorthand for the moment a supposedly invulnerable platform encounters a complete enemy system. The phrase is later applied to naval, orbital, and computing failures even when no aircraft are involved.</p>"}
    ],related:americanMissionRelated.filter(item=>item.href!=="maracaibo-shock.html").concat([{href:"military-technology.html",kicker:"Technology context",label:"Military Technology"},{href:"aerospace-doctrine.html",kicker:"Operational context",label:"Aerospace Doctrine"},{href:"united-states.html",kicker:"National setting",label:"United States"}]),sources:americanAerospaceSources,
    categories:["United States Aerospace Force","Venezuela","Air defense","Military doctrine","1966"],
    facts:[["Year","1966"],["Location","Near Maracaibo, Venezuela"],["American losses","Two B-58s and one suppression aircraft"],["Defending technology","German-designed radar and surface-to-air system"],["Campaign outcome","American victory"],["Doctrine disproved","Safety through speed and altitude"],["Immediate response","Electronic warfare and route planning"],["Long-term response","Greater investment in orbital aerospace"]]
  },

  "establishment-compact": {
    title:"Establishment Compact",category:"Politics",eyebrow:"1968 contingent election · House of Representatives",infoboxKicker:"American political agreement",infoboxTitle:"Establishment Compact",
    lead:"The Establishment Compact was the congressional agreement by which Democratic and Republican state delegations prevented George Lincoln Rockwell from winning the contingent presidential election of 1968 and selected Nelson Rockefeller instead. Constitutionally regular and politically incendiary, it created National Renewal's <a href='stolen-mandate.html'>Stolen Mandate</a> myth and accelerated the fusion of its opponents.",
    canon:"The purpose, participating parties, Rockefeller selection, Senate bargain, and political consequences are established. The Democratic nominee, individual delegation votes, exact House ballot, and vice-presidential identity remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"election",title:"The election without a majority",html:"<p>Rockwell wins roughly forty percent of the popular vote and the largest electoral plurality but lacks an Electoral College majority. Under the Twelfth Amendment the House chooses among the leading candidates, with each state delegation casting one vote.</p>"},
      {id:"bargain",title:"The Democratic–Republican bargain",html:"<p>Democrats and Republicans conclude independently that neither can permit a National Renewal executive. Rockefeller has more electoral support than the Democratic candidate and is less objectionable to Democratic delegations than Rockwell.</p><p>Democratic states therefore vote Rockefeller or align their delegation procedure so that he can secure the required majority.</p>"},
      {id:"ballot",title:"The House and Senate ballots",html:"<p>The exact state-delegation division remains open, but the Establishment Compact supplies the majority required to select Rockefeller. The Senate makes a parallel cross-party arrangement and selects the Republican vice-presidential candidate.</p>"},
      {id:"legality",title:"Legality and legitimacy",html:"<p>Supporters answer that plurality is not majority and that the constitutional procedure exists precisely for a divided election. No state delegation is compelled and no formal rule is suspended.</p><p>National Renewal answers that it won both the popular and electoral pluralities and that two nominally opposed parties united only when voters threatened their shared control.</p>"},
      {id:"myth",title:"The Stolen Mandate",html:"<p><a href='stolen-mandate.html'>“Stolen Mandate,” “House Betrayal,” and “Establishment Coup”</a> become foundational National Renewal language. The episode converts procedural defeat into proof that the older parties form one regime beneath their competition.</p>"},
      {id:"rockefeller",title:"The Rockefeller presidency",html:"<p>Rockefeller enters office legally but without a popular or electoral plurality. Dependent upon a shifting anti-Rockwell coalition, he acts as a stabilizer: avoiding new interventions, preserving markets, calming allies, and seeking ordinary constitutional legitimacy.</p><p>The restraint contrasts sharply with Kennedy's mission politics and makes the administration appear motionless to a public accustomed to achievement.</p>"},
      {id:"fusion",title:"Party fusion",html:"<p>Congress becomes a three-party battlefield. Continued cooperation against National Renewal gradually turns an electoral compact into institutional fusion, producing the Democratic-Republican Party by 1972.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Legacy",html:"<p>The compact remains the central American dispute over whether constitutional legality can itself become anti-democratic. Buchanan's certification of Reagan's victory in 1984 is celebrated partly because it rejects the expectation that every lost election must be interpreted through 1968.</p>"}
    ],related:americanMissionRelated.filter(item=>item.href!=="establishment-compact.html").concat([{href:"1968-united-states-presidential-election.html",kicker:"Contingent election",label:"1968 Presidential Election"},{href:"stolen-mandate.html",kicker:"Renewal interpretation",label:"Stolen Mandate"},{href:"american-parties.html",kicker:"Party history",label:"Political Parties in the United States"},{href:"conspiracy-republic.html",kicker:"Cultural aftermath",label:"Conspiracy Republic"}]),sources:americanMissionSources,
    categories:["United States","1968 election","Contingent elections","National Renewal","Democratic-Republican Party"],
    facts:[["Date","1968–1969"],["Arena","House of Representatives"],["Constitutional basis","Twelfth Amendment contingent election"],["Purpose","Prevent a Rockwell presidency"],["Selected president","Nelson Rockefeller"],["House result","Rockefeller majority; exact ballot open"],["Renewal name for outcome","Stolen Mandate"],["Long-term result","Democratic–Republican fusion"]]
  },

  "national-mission-productive-enterprise-act": {
    title:"National Mission and Productive Enterprise Act",category:"Economy",eyebrow:"1977 · Buchanan administration · industrial mission",infoboxKicker:"United States federal legislation",infoboxTitle:"National Mission and Productive Enterprise Act of 1977",
    lead:"The National Mission and Productive Enterprise Act was the principal legislation of Pat Buchanan's first year. It joined Orbital Port Columbia, permanent lunar planning, military spaceplanes, microelectronics, machine tools, industrial credit, domestic procurement, retraining, and technical institutes under the formula “Peace, production, and ascent.”",
    canon:"The 1977 act, principal programs, Senate removal of compulsory corporatist provisions, industrial-credit approach, automation disappointment, and role in personal computing are established. Exact section numbering, appropriations, and institutional names below the national level remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"passage",title:"Passage under divided government",html:"<p>National Renewal holds the presidency and a narrow House majority while Democratic-Republicans retain the Senate. Buchanan presents the measure as visible national work rather than constitutional revolution. Senate bargaining removes mandatory industrial organization and preserves a mixed private system.</p>"},
      {id:"space",title:"Ascent",html:"<p>The act authorizes Orbital Port Columbia, permanent lunar-base planning, expansion of the runway-to-orbit fleet, orbital security, and scientific infrastructure. A classified annex creates <a href='project-zenith.html'>Project Zenith</a>.</p>"},
      {id:"industry",title:"Productive enterprise",html:"<p>Industrial provisions support microelectronics, numerical-control machine tools, programmable controllers, semiconductor equipment, computerized inventory, domestic procurement, worker retraining, and technical institutes. Federal guarantees encourage local and regional banks to lend for approved American equipment.</p>"},
      {id:"credit",title:"Productive and speculative credit",html:"<p>National Renewal distinguishes credit for factories, farms, housing, machines, computers, power, and transport from credit for asset manipulation, stripping, luxury imports, and short-term trading. The distinction is politically powerful even where particular loans do not fit cleanly into either category.</p>"},
      {id:"institutes",title:"National Production Institutes",html:"<p>A network of institutes trains machinists, programmers, tool setters, maintenance workers, inspectors, and production engineers. The institutions connect vocational schooling to federal procurement and local industrial redevelopment.</p>"},
      {id:"automation",title:"The automation disappointment",html:"<p>Promoters promise workerless factories in which raw material enters and finished goods emerge under a few technicians. Actual German and Japanese automation still depends upon skilled maintenance, tooling, programming, inspection, and logistics.</p><p>Some subsidized American firms buy equipment they cannot operate, duplicate projects without markets, or survive through political connections.</p>"},
      {id:"computing",title:"The least planned success",html:"<p>Personal computing becomes the act's most culturally important result even though it is not centrally designed. Semiconductor research, school and federal purchasing, small-business deductions, telecommunications, and user clubs create a market for software, spreadsheets, word processing, games, and home programming.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Reform and legacy",html:"<p>The program builds real industrial depth and visible aerospace achievement while producing favoritism and an exaggerated belief that capital equipment automatically creates competence. The 1982 recession leads to the <a href='american-productive-commonwealth-act.html'>American Productive Commonwealth Act</a>, which conditions further aid rather than abandoning the mission.</p>"}
    ],related:americanMissionRelated.filter(item=>item.href!=="national-mission-productive-enterprise-act.html").concat([{href:"computing.html",kicker:"Technical consequence",label:"Computing"},{href:"orbital-stations.html",kicker:"Orbital project",label:"Orbital Stations"},{href:"american-cottage-industry-revival.html",kicker:"Household consequence",label:"Cottage-Industry Revival"}]),sources:americanMissionSources,
    categories:["United States","Economic policy","Industrial policy","Space policy","Pat Buchanan"],
    facts:[["Enacted","1977"],["President","Pat Buchanan"],["Governing formula","Peace, production, and ascent"],["Orbital project","Orbital Port Columbia"],["Classified program","Project Zenith"],["Industrial tools","Machine tools · controls · microelectronics"],["Education system","National Production Institutes"],["Later reform","American Productive Commonwealth Act · 1982"]]
  },

  "project-zenith": {
    title:"Project Zenith",category:"Military",eyebrow:"1977–1985 · military spaceplanes · orbital security",infoboxKicker:"Classified American aerospace program",infoboxTitle:"Project Zenith",
    lead:"Project Zenith is the classified American O-7 military spaceplane and orbital-security program authorized in 1977. Operated by Orbital Security Command of the United States Aerospace Force, it combines the high-speed interceptor and bomber line with first-generation O-series experience for inspection, interception, rescue, electronic warfare, controlled deorbit, reconnaissance, and secondary guided conventional strategic attack.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>",
    canon:"The 1977 authorization, Aerospace Force ownership, O-7A limited readiness in 1984, distinct military lineage within the O series, mission set, and conventional weapons doctrine are established. Exact fleet size, basing pattern, and most missions remain classified or open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"authorization",title:"Authorization",html:"<p>A classified annex to the <a href='national-mission-productive-enterprise-act.html'>National Mission and Productive Enterprise Act</a> authorizes Zenith in 1977. The public orbital-port program and the military project share industrial and maintenance infrastructure from the beginning.</p>"},
      {id:"command",title:"Orbital Security Command",html:"<p>Zenith is not a separate agency. Orbital Security Command within the <a href='united-states-aerospace-force.html'>Aerospace Force</a> controls its vehicles, reconnaissance systems, protected facilities, crews, and operational planning.</p><p>Columbia remains formally noncombat infrastructure; armed Zenith vehicles operate from segregated complexes rather than docking armed beside civilian habitation.</p>"},
      {id:"vehicle",title:"O-7A Zenith",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-h6t3c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T11:52:57-06:00 --><p>O-7A is not an armed Columbia derivative. Its design ancestry runs through the F-19 and B-70 high-speed line, combined with first-generation O-series knowledge of reentry, runway recovery, thermal protection, avionics, and servicing. It adds protected communications, maneuvering propellant, cross-range, electronic warfare, classified navigation, and an internal mission bay.</p><p>It reaches limited readiness in 1984 with a two-person crew and remotely supervised modes for hazardous missions.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"missions",title:"Mission set",html:"<p>Declared missions include orbital inspection, rescue, recovery, electronic support, controlled deorbit of dangerous objects, and protection of American stations. Classified planning includes interference with hostile sensors, close approach to military satellites, and conventional strategic attack.</p>"},
      {id:"weapons",title:"Conventional orbital weapons",html:"<p>Zenith doctrine emphasizes guided conventional reentry bodies, anti-satellite mechanisms designed to limit debris, electronic attack, and mission kills rather than radiological dispersal or indiscriminate fragmentation. The 1,250-kilogram <a href='grp-1-sledge.html'>GRP-1 Sledge</a> reaches limited readiness in 1984 for hardened point targets. The 2,000-kilogram <a href='orb-2.html'>ORB-2</a> enters trials in 1985 as a conventional high-explosive weapon for airfields, ports, radar, and command facilities.</p><p>The 1974 mass-effect convention does not classify conventional kinetic penetrators as weapons of mass effect. Neither weapon provides instantaneous global attack: release depends upon controlled corridors and suitable orbital geometry.</p>"},
      {id:"crisis",title:"The approach crisis of 1981–1982",html:"<p>A close American approach to a German military satellite produces an orbital crisis. British and Italian mediation leads to the Geneva Orbital Incidents Agreement: transponders, approach notice, safety zones, hotlines, rescue duties, and protection of life support without demilitarization.</p>"},
      {id:"secrecy",title:"Secrecy and public knowledge",html:"<p>The existence of a protected military orbital program becomes broadly understood before its technical details are admitted. Public debate turns on whether rescue and inspection can be separated from coercive approach and whether an orbital post remains scientific when the same service maintains strategic vehicles nearby.</p>"},
      {id:"balance",title:"Place in the 1985 balance",html:"<p>Zenith gives the United States unmatched reusable inspection and return capability, but not dominance of every orbit. Germany possesses armed Raumjäger and high-orbit infrastructure; Japan relies more heavily upon distributed optical and robotic control; Britain and the Latin states use law, tracking, and coalition access to compensate for smaller forces.</p>"}
    ],related:americanMissionRelated.filter(item=>item.href!=="project-zenith.html").concat([{href:"space-programs.html",kicker:"Strategic setting",label:"Space Race"},{href:"orbital-weapons.html",kicker:"Weapons history",label:"Orbital Weapons"},{href:"orbital-law.html",kicker:"Legal limits",label:"Orbital Law"}]),sources:americanAerospaceSources,
    categories:["United States Aerospace Force","Military spaceplanes","Orbital warfare","Classified programs","1977"],
    facts:[["Authorized","1977"],["Parent service","United States Aerospace Force"],["Command","Orbital Security Command"],["Operational vehicle","O-7A Zenith"],["Readiness","Limited · 1984"],["Crew","Two"],["Primary missions","Inspection · interception · rescue · electronic warfare"],["Strategic mission","Secondary guided conventional orbital attack"]]
  },

  "rockwell-commission": {
    title:"Rockwell Commission",category:"Politics",eyebrow:"1977–1978 · assassination inquiry · wrongful execution",infoboxKicker:"United States presidential commission",infoboxTitle:"Commission on the Assassination of George Lincoln Rockwell",
    lead:"The Rockwell Commission was the bipartisan presidential inquiry appointed by Pat Buchanan to review the prosecution and execution of the man officially blamed for George Lincoln Rockwell's 1975 assassination. Its 1978 report found the confession unreliable, contrary evidence suppressed, and the executed suspect probably innocent, but did not identify the real assassin.",
    canon:"The commission's appointment, bipartisan composition, 1978 findings, posthumous relief, and failure to identify the assassin are established. Commissioner names, suspect identity, exact evidentiary record, and report title remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"case",title:"The official assassination case",html:"<p>Rockwell is <a href='assassination-of-george-lincoln-rockwell.html'>killed by a trained rifleman</a> during the first major speech of his 1976 campaign tour. The true assassin escapes. The FBI builds a case against an anti-Rockwell eccentric through a disputed confession and weak physical evidence.</p><p>Accelerated capital proceedings end in execution before the contradictions receive sustained public review.</p>"},
      {id:"appointment",title:"Buchanan's appointment",html:"<p>One of Buchanan's first executive actions is creation of the Commission on the Assassination of George Lincoln Rockwell. Its official purpose is to determine whether the conviction was sound, not to prove that the older parties or a foreign state ordered the murder.</p>"},
      {id:"composition",title:"Bipartisan composition",html:"<p>The commission includes Democratic-Republican jurists, National Renewal members, forensic experts, retired judges, former law-enforcement officers, and representatives acceptable to Rockwell's family. The balance satisfies the party's demand for inquiry while limiting claims that the commission is a purge.</p>"},
      {id:"evidence",title:"Review of evidence",html:"<p>Investigators reconstruct the confession process, chain of custody, ballistics, witness statements, withheld material, and pressure for rapid closure. The record shows that contrary evidence was suppressed and that the physical case did not support the certainty claimed at trial.</p>"},
      {id:"findings",title:"The 1978 findings",html:"<p>The report concludes that the confession was unreliable, investigators suppressed contrary evidence, and the executed man was probably innocent. It does not establish the identity, motive, or institutional affiliation of the actual rifleman.</p>"},
      {id:"relief",title:"Relief and released records",html:"<p>The government releases substantial records and grants compensation or posthumous relief to the suspect's family. Officials responsible for misconduct face professional and political consequences, though the open criminal case produces no replacement defendant.</p>"},
      {id:"reaction",title:"Political reaction",html:"<p>National Renewal receives vindication without proof of an establishment assassination conspiracy. The old guard treats uncertainty as evidence of deeper concealment; Buchananites emphasize that lawful inquiry corrected state injustice without revolutionary retaliation.</p><p>Democratic-Republicans accept the wrongful-case finding while rejecting claims that the compact of 1968 caused the murder.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Legacy",html:"<p>The commission deepens the <a href='conspiracy-republic.html'>Conspiracy Republic</a> because it proves official deception while leaving the central mystery unresolved. It also becomes a constitutional precedent for reviewing politically charged convictions without declaring every failure evidence of one omnipotent conspiracy.</p>"}
    ],related:americanMissionRelated.filter(item=>item.href!=="rockwell-commission.html").concat([{href:"assassination-of-george-lincoln-rockwell.html",kicker:"Event under review",label:"Assassination of George Lincoln Rockwell"},{href:"last-republic.html",kicker:"Cultural background",label:"The Last Republic"},{href:"presidents.html",kicker:"Executive context",label:"List of U.S. Presidents"},{href:"american-parties.html",kicker:"Party setting",label:"Political Parties in the United States"}]),sources:americanMissionSources,
    categories:["George Lincoln Rockwell","Pat Buchanan","Presidential commissions","Wrongful convictions","1978"],
    facts:[["Appointed","1977"],["Report","1978"],["Appointing president","Pat Buchanan"],["Subject","Rockwell assassination prosecution"],["Confession finding","Unreliable"],["Evidence finding","Contrary material suppressed"],["Conviction finding","Executed suspect probably innocent"],["Assassin identified","No"]]
  },

  "national-unity-amendment": {
    title:"National Unity and Constitutional Defense Amendment",category:"Politics",eyebrow:"1981 · failed constitutional proposal · National Renewal",infoboxKicker:"Proposed United States amendment",infoboxTitle:"National Unity and Constitutional Defense Amendment",
    lead:"The National Unity and Constitutional Defense Amendment was a failed 1981 proposal by Rockwellian and radical-corporatist legislators to register and discipline national parties, suspend foreign-directed organizations, establish occupational representation, and synchronize political life during emergencies. Pat Buchanan's rejection fixed the constitutional boundary of Buchananism.",
    canon:"The 1981 proposal, principal provisions, limited sponsorship, Buchanan's quoted rejection, committee death, and factional consequences are established. Formal text, sponsors, committee name, and vote totals remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"proposal",title:"The proposal",html:"<p>A small caucus introduces the amendment after National Renewal's 1980 landslide. Its authors claim that electoral success creates an opportunity to protect the republic permanently against foreign-directed and sectional organization.</p>"},
      {id:"parties",title:"Registration and loyalty",html:"<p>The proposal requires federal registration of national political organizations and loyalty conditions for ballot access. It grants authority to suspend organizations accused of foreign direction and restricts parties organized principally around class, race, region, or foreign ideology.</p>"},
      {id:"corporate",title:"Occupational representation",html:"<p>A permanent National Council would represent labor, industry, agriculture, veterans, churches, and professions. During emergencies, political and occupational organizations could be synchronized into one recognized national framework.</p>"},
      {id:"support",title:"Limited support",html:"<p>The amendment attracts roughly thirty to forty representatives and a handful of senators. Most National Renewal legislators refuse sponsorship despite nominal control of Congress, recognizing both constitutional risk and the threat occupational bodies would pose to ordinary committee power.</p>"},
      {id:"buchanan",title:"Buchanan's rejection",html:"<p>Buchanan declares, “We were given the Republic to restore, not to replace.” He refuses suspension of the Democratic-Republicans, merger of party and civil service, permanent emergency power, a federal party militia, or treatment of Rockwell's writings as state doctrine.</p>"},
      {id:"death",title:"Death in committee",html:"<p>Presidential opposition deprives the proposal of mainstream sponsorship. It dies in committee without a House vote and never approaches the supermajorities required for an amendment.</p>"},
      {id:"factions",title:"The party revealed",html:"<p>The episode distinguishes constitutional Buchananites, mission technocrats, and congressional nationalists from the revolutionary old guard. Success has attached many former insurgents to districts, committee chairs, patronage, contracts, and the existing electoral rules.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Constitutional legacy",html:"<p>The rejection reassures voters who supported National Renewal for industrial and space achievement rather than permanent party rule. During the 1984 debate Howard Phillips's hesitation over the amendment lets Reagan argue that a successful government does not inherit the republic.</p>"}
    ],related:americanMissionRelated.filter(item=>item.href!=="national-unity-amendment.html").concat([{href:"american-parties.html",kicker:"Party setting",label:"Political Parties in the United States"},{href:"five-party-republic.html",kicker:"Later constitutional order",label:"Five-Party Republic"},{href:"presidents.html",kicker:"Executive context",label:"List of U.S. Presidents"}]),sources:americanMissionSources,
    categories:["National Renewal","United States Constitution","Pat Buchanan","Corporatism","1981"],
    facts:[["Introduced","1981"],["Type","Proposed constitutional amendment"],["Sponsors","Rockwellian and radical-corporatist caucus"],["House support","Approximately 30–40 representatives"],["Core mechanisms","Party registration · suspension · occupational council"],["Presidential position","Opposed"],["Defining response","We were given the Republic to restore, not to replace"],["Outcome","Died in committee"]]
  },

  "american-productive-commonwealth-act": {
    title:"American Productive Commonwealth Act",category:"Economy",eyebrow:"1982 · industrial reform · productivity recession",infoboxKicker:"United States federal legislation",infoboxTitle:"American Productive Commonwealth Act of 1982",
    lead:"The American Productive Commonwealth Act was the 1982 reform of National Renewal's industrial-credit system. Passed during a short productivity recession, it conditioned federal assistance on technical competence, training, domestic production, independent audit, realistic demand, and limits on private extraction while providing portable retraining accounts to displaced workers.",
    canon:"The 1982 recession, act, principal conditions, worker accounts, consolidation policy, and broad recovery effect are established. Exact statutory agencies, appropriations, and firm-level cases remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"recession",title:"The productivity recession",html:"<p>By 1981 some firms cannot operate subsidized robots, politically connected borrowers depend on automatic refinancing, semiconductor projects duplicate one another, and managers expect every computer purchase to produce immediate profit.</p><p>Modest monetary tightening and stricter lending create a short 1982 recession. Weak firms fail, unemployment rises temporarily, inflation falls, and stronger firms acquire equipment and trained workers.</p>"},
      {id:"purpose",title:"Reform rather than indiscriminate expansion",html:"<p>Buchanan refuses to save every company calling itself patriotic. The act preserves productive credit while replacing political enthusiasm with evidence that a borrower can use equipment, train labor, and serve a real market.</p>"},
      {id:"conditions",title:"Conditions on assistance",html:"<p>Federally assisted firms must demonstrate technical competence, worker-training plans, domestic production, independent auditing, realistic demand, and limits on executive extraction of subsidized profits. Refinancing becomes conditional rather than automatic.</p>"},
      {id:"workers",title:"Portable training accounts",html:"<p>Workers displaced by failure or consolidation receive portable training accounts and preference for hiring into other federally supported industries. The benefit follows the worker rather than obliging the government to preserve a particular employer.</p>"},
      {id:"consolidation",title:"Failure and consolidation",html:"<p>Viable firms purchase machine tools, controllers, inventories, patents, and facilities from failed borrowers. The policy aims to preserve productive capacity without preserving every ownership claim or management team.</p>"},
      {id:"politics",title:"Political argument",html:"<p>Mission technocrats welcome competence rules; old corporatists argue that national solidarity requires stronger rescue; Democratic-Republicans claim the failures prove political credit allocation is inherently corrupt. Buchanan presents the act as evidence that mission requires judgment rather than protection from consequence.</p>"},
      {id:"results",title:"Results by 1984",html:"<p>The economy is not miraculous, but manufacturing remains deep in automobiles, aircraft, electronics, machinery, chemicals, shipbuilding, appliances, computers, and armaments. Inflation falls and new technology remains associated with growth rather than national decline.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Legacy",html:"<p>The act becomes the mature form of Buchananite industrial policy: private firms, public strategic direction, conditioned credit, worker mobility, and domestic capacity. Reagan later reduces party-directed allocation while retaining technical education, protected strategic sectors, and many audit standards.</p>"}
    ],related:americanMissionRelated.filter(item=>item.href!=="american-productive-commonwealth-act.html").concat([{href:"political-economy.html",kicker:"Comparative setting",label:"Political Economy by Bloc"},{href:"computing.html",kicker:"Industrial technology",label:"Computing"},{href:"reagan-era.html",kicker:"Successor policy",label:"Reagan Era"}]),sources:americanMissionSources,
    categories:["United States","Industrial policy","Economic recessions","National Renewal","1982"],
    facts:[["Enacted","1982"],["President","Pat Buchanan"],["Trigger","Productivity recession"],["Policy direction","Conditioned industrial assistance"],["Firm requirements","Competence · training · audit · demand"],["Worker provision","Portable retraining accounts"],["Failure policy","Permit failure and productive consolidation"],["Successor approach","Selective retention under Reagan"]]
  },

  "narrative-shaping-memorandum": {
    title:"Narrative Shaping memorandum",category:"Politics",eyebrow:"1983 leak · political communication · 1984 campaign",infoboxKicker:"Leaked National Renewal document",infoboxTitle:"Narrative Shaping for the Post-Buchanan Transition",
    lead:"“Narrative Shaping for the Post-Buchanan Transition” was a leaked 1983 National Renewal communications memorandum proposing coordination among government announcements, documentaries, party speakers, industry, friendly broadcasters, schools, churches, family organizations, and patriotic entertainment. Its bureaucratic language made ordinary political communication appear to be a program for managing public understanding.",
    canon:"The 1983 memorandum, title, broad contents, damaging phrases, electronic circulation, Reagan's use of the leak, and the later sarcastic meaning of 'narrative shaping' are established. The issuing office and original drafter remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"origin",title:"Origin",html:"<p>The memorandum comes from either the party's Office of Public Confidence or its National Communications Secretariat during planning for the transition from Buchanan to Howard Phillips. The unresolved office name reflects uncertainty in the surviving administrative record rather than doubt that the document existed.</p>"},
      {id:"contents",title:"Proposed coordination",html:"<p>The paper recommends coordinating cabinet announcements, space documentaries, party speakers, industrial associations, friendly broadcasters, school material, church and family organizations, patriotic entertainment, and responses to foreign reporting.</p><p>It does not disclose a secret censorship service. Most listed activities are legal forms of government and campaign communication.</p>"},
      {id:"language",title:"The language problem",html:"<p>Terms such as “narrative shaping,” “managed public understanding,” “message synchronization,” “trusted cultural intermediaries,” and “preemption of hostile interpretive frames” sound like euphemisms for instructing citizens how to understand facts rather than presenting the facts themselves.</p>"},
      {id:"leak",title:"The 1983 leak",html:"<p>Copies circulate through congressional offices, journalists, universities, local bulletin boards, electronic newspapers, and the expanding American Data Network. Decentralized copying allows the phrases to become jokes and slogans before National Renewal can provide one authoritative interpretation.</p>"},
      {id:"reagan",title:"Reagan's attack",html:"<p>Reagan does not claim Buchanan is a dictator. He argues that a long-governing party can no longer distinguish between telling Americans what it has done and telling them how they must understand it.</p><p>His line—“They call it narrative shaping. Most Americans still call it making up your mind for you”—translates administrative language into a constitutional criticism.</p>"},
      {id:"phillips",title:"Phillips's defense",html:"<p>Howard Phillips answers that every government coordinates information and that hostile blocs openly conduct propaganda. The technically correct defense reinforces the political damage because it fails to address why schools, churches, entertainment, industry, and government appear inside one communications plan.</p>"},
      {id:"network",title:"A network-era scandal",html:"<p>The leak demonstrates that decentralized electronic circulation has outgrown National Renewal's ability to manage national explanation. A document produced by a mass-broadcast organization is disassembled, quoted, parodied, and recontextualized by thousands of small publishers and users.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Legacy of the phrase",html:"<p>After 1984 “narrative shaping” becomes a sarcastic American synonym for propaganda presented as neutral explanation. The expression outlives the election and is applied to corporate publicity, foreign fronts, school controversies, and every later administration.</p>"}
    ],related:americanMissionRelated.filter(item=>item.href!=="narrative-shaping-memorandum.html").concat([{href:"propaganda-intelligence.html",kicker:"Comparative information conflict",label:"Propaganda and Cultural Infiltration"},{href:"computing-networks.html",kicker:"Distribution system",label:"Computing Networks"},{href:"american-elections.html",kicker:"Campaign setting",label:"American Presidential Elections"}]),sources:americanMissionSources,
    categories:["National Renewal","Political communication","Propaganda","American Data Network","1983"],
    facts:[["Date","1983"],["Document","Internal communications memorandum"],["Possible issuer","Office of Public Confidence or National Communications Secretariat"],["Political context","Post-Buchanan succession"],["Leak medium","Press and American Data Network"],["Principal critic","Ronald Reagan"],["Immediate effect","Damage to Phillips's 1984 campaign"],["Later meaning","Sarcastic synonym for managed propaganda"]]
  }
});
